WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.363 --> 00:00:01.560
<v Instructor>So let's talk about</v>

2
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.450
some real-time architectures

3
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.310
for your full data engineering pipeline.

4
00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.350
So we can produce data, for example,

5
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.420
into Amazon Kinesis data streams,

6
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.950
and then we can build several things out of it.

7
00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.560
We can build an Amazon EC2 based application

8
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.850
to read from your stream

9
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.950
and your full power over what you do.

10
00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:18.540
You can also build an application

11
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.670
directly under Apache Flink

12
00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:21.900
for the managed service.

13
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.970
So this is for streaming analytics,

14
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.130
lots of data transformations and so on.

15
00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.600
It's a very great framework

16
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.480
and the managed version on AWS is very handy,

17
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.225
or you can just sync this data into various targets.

18
00:00:33.225 --> 00:00:34.230
And for this,

19
00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.630
the best is to use Amazon data Firehose

20
00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.060
in which you can send, for example, to Amazon S3

21
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.843
and through Amazon S3.

22
00:00:40.843 --> 00:00:45.090
A very popular target is Amazon Redshift,

23
00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:46.200
but you can also send it,

24
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.720
for example, to Amazon open search service.

25
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.400
Finally, you should know

26
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.550
that you can also produce directly into Amazon Data Firehose

27
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.383
if you want to.

28
00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.320
Another architectural discussion we may have is,

29
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.870
we have different things to do streaming in AWS.

30
00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.210
Which one is going to be the more cost effective?

31
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:04.110
So let's say for example,

32
00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.750
that you plan on receiving 3000 messages

33
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.940
of one kilobytes per second.

34
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.320
So this is all your data

35
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.660
going to be sent to your architecture.

36
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.810
So option number one is to use Kinesis data streams

37
00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.490
with a Lambda function.

38
00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:21.390
And option number two is going to be using a DynamoDB table

39
00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.330
with a DynamoDB streams and a Lambda function.

40
00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.630
So ask yourself what is going to be the most efficient

41
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.610
and what is going to be the most cost effective,

42
00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.440
and what trade-offs are we going to have

43
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.710
between these two architectures because they both work.

44
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.440
Well for Kinesis, I did some computations for you.

45
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.500
So we have three shards because we need 3000 messages

46
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.760
of one kilobytes.

47
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.310
So three shards are gonna be three megabytes per second,

48
00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:49.140
and the cost of three shards per hour is three times 0.015.

49
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.260
This is based on some numbers I found on the AWS websites,

50
00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:57.260
and this gives you about $32 per month of cost.

51
00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.870
Then to send the data into Lambda, this is very easy.

52
00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.790
We can just use an event mapper

53
00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:06.210
and if we wanted to send the data for long-term storage,

54
00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.670
we would need to use something like Kinesis Data Firehose.

55
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:12.300
I'll put it into S3 and we would get long-term storage there

56
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.050
because can see the streams will have 24 hours to one year

57
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:19.380
of data retention, but after that, the data will be lost.

58
00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.540
So this is a good combo to use KDF

59
00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.400
to persist the data into S3

60
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.920
very cheaply for long-term storage.

61
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.220
Next, when you look at DynamoDB and DynamoDB streams,

62
00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.910
we need to have 3000 writes of one kilobyte per second.

63
00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:37.830
So if you remember, that is 3000 WCU, write capacity units,

64
00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.110
and that gives you three megabytes per second.

65
00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:43.440
Now, if you do a computation of how much that cost you,

66
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.440
this cost you about 1,450 US dollars per month

67
00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.470
just for that DynamoDB table.

68
00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:54.120
So the good thing is that yes,

69
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.010
we do get storage in DynamoDB,

70
00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.230
so the data can be stored long term there,

71
00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:01.470
but the streaming aspect of things is extremely expensive.

72
00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:04.312
So there's a factor 50 of cost between Kinesis

73
00:03:04.312 --> 00:03:06.360
and DynamoDB plus DynamoDB streams.

74
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.100
So this is something you have to realize

75
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.270
as a solution architect.

76
00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.830
It's not because something works,

77
00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:12.600
but it's going to be a great solution.

78
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.230
And so in this example, both solutions work for streaming,

79
00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.770
but Kinesis is obviously going to be a much cheaper

80
00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.713
and probably better choice for what you're trying to do.

81
00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:27.167
Okay, so now let's look at all the technologies

82
00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:29.190
we've seen so far for data exchange

83
00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.530
and we can understand where are the different aspects

84
00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.800
based on data retention, ordering, scalability,

85
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.260
the possible readers and latency.

86
00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.200
So Kinesis data streams is immutable data,

87
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.330
so once it's inserted, we cannot change it

88
00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:44.820
and the retention is up to one year.

89
00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.450
We can export to S3 using Kinesis Data Firehose.

90
00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.250
The ordering is going to be per shard,

91
00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.200
and we have to provision the shards in advance.

92
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.260
And the readers for the shards can be EC2 instances,

93
00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.690
Lambda function, Kinesis Data Firehose,

94
00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:00.870
Kinesis Data analytics, and KCL, Kinesis Client Library

95
00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:02.700
that has a checkpointing feature.

96
00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.880
In terms of latency, if we're using Kinesis data streams,

97
00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.130
then we have about 200 millisecond latency.

98
00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.930
And if we're using Kinesis Data Firehose,

99
00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.303
it's not latency, it's near real time,

100
00:04:11.303 --> 00:04:13.860
so it's about going to be one minute minimum

101
00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.480
to flush that buffer size

102
00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.720
in that buffer into Amazon S3 for example.

103
00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.270
Next, let's look at SQS.

104
00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:22.620
So SQS, the data is also immutable,

105
00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.410
once it's inserted, it cannot be removed,

106
00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:26.910
and the data retention in SQS

107
00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.970
is going to be between 1-14 days.

108
00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.340
There is no ordering for SQS standard,

109
00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:34.890
and the scalability is virtually unlimited.

110
00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.530
There's a soft limit, but you can always increase it.

111
00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.880
The readers of SQS is going to be EC2 or Lambda functions,

112
00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.190
and the latency in this case is going to be quite low

113
00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:47.160
between 10 to 100 milliseconds.

114
00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.160
Then we have SQS FIFO, which is very similar to SQS,

115
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.810
but the data is still immutable.

116
00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.250
We still have the same retention,

117
00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.860
but now there's an ordering we can have thanks to FIFO,

118
00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.450
which means "First In First Out."

119
00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.760
And so we get ordering per group id.

120
00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.680
Now, because we have this ordering constraints,

121
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.410
the scalability is going to be different.

122
00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.140
So we have about 300 messages per second

123
00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.500
or three thousands if we're using batching by batching

124
00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.070
10 messages at a time.

125
00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:14.130
The readers can also be EC2 and Lambda function,

126
00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.450
and the latency will be quite low

127
00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.910
between 10 to 100 milliseconds.

128
00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:21.090
Then we have SNS, which is also going to be immutable data.

129
00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.486
There's no data retention whatsoever, no ordering.

130
00:05:23.486 --> 00:05:26.370
This is going to be also something that scales really well.

131
00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.830
So there's a self limit, but you can always increase it.

132
00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:31.050
And in terms of what can consume from SNS,

133
00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:32.370
these are called subscribers.

134
00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.980
So it can be an HTTP subscriber, Lambda function,

135
00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:38.136
an email or SQSQ if we're using find out patterns,

136
00:05:38.136 --> 00:05:39.870
this kind of stuff.

137
00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.460
And the latency is going to be quite low

138
00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.967
between 10 to 100 milliseconds.

139
00:05:43.967 --> 00:05:47.340
DynamoDB now is going to be having mutable data,

140
00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:50.910
so once we insert data into DynamoDB, we can override it.

141
00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.770
The retention of the data can be infinite,

142
00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:56.130
or we can implement a time to live on our rows.

143
00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:58.200
There is no ordering in DynamoDB.

144
00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:02.790
For scalability, we have to use either some WCU and RCU

145
00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.052
to create and provision our database

146
00:06:05.052 --> 00:06:07.860
and maybe have auto scaling on top of it,

147
00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:09.390
or we can use on demand scaling

148
00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.850
if we don't know our usage pattern in advance

149
00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.440
and we have something very spiky,

150
00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.140
very unpredictable, or very, very, very low.

151
00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:18.840
Finally, in terms of what can read from a DynamoDB,

152
00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:19.920
well, you have the SDK,

153
00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:21.810
but you cannot read a stream using the SDK.

154
00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.760
You need to create first a DynamoDB stream,

155
00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.680
and then that DynamoDB stream

156
00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.510
can be read from a Lambda function

157
00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.460
or an EC2 instance, for example.

158
00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.020
The latency is also quite low

159
00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:33.660
between 10 to 100 milliseconds.

160
00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:36.960
And finally, S3, yes, S3 is a way to stream data

161
00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:38.820
if you wanted to, if you're a bit twisted.

162
00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.670
So you can insert data in S3 and replace it.

163
00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:43.350
So there is a mutable aspect,

164
00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:45.510
but we've seen that there is S3 object logs

165
00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.760
or versioning that can help against that.

166
00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:49.680
The retention is going to be infinite,

167
00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.240
but we can set up lifecycle policies

168
00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.130
to transition between different tiers.

169
00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:57.510
And we can also have a lifecycle policy to delete data.

170
00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:59.580
There's no ordering in S3.

171
00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:03.060
For scalability, we get about 3,500 puts per second

172
00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.550
and 5,500 gets per second per prefix,

173
00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.920
and the storage itself is infinite.

174
00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:10.650
And then the readers of S3 can be the SDK

175
00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:13.560
to get a specific object.

176
00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:14.850
Or we can use S3 events

177
00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:18.510
to get notified whenever an object is inserted into S3.

178
00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:20.550
In terms of latency, it is quite low,

179
00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.310
so between 10 to 100 milliseconds.

180
00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:24.143
So that's it.

181
00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:24.976
Hopefully that was helpful

182
00:07:24.976 --> 00:07:26.400
from an architectural perspective.

183
00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:27.330
Hopefully it's very clear

184
00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:29.760
whether the differences between all these technologies

185
00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.770
and you will be able to answer the questions

186
00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:33.600
based on them at the exam.

187
00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.350
Alright, that's it. I will see you in the next lecture.

